---
export const prerender = false;
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getSupabaseAdmin } from "../../../lib/supabase";
import FaqAccordion from "../../../components/FaqAccordion.astro";
import ProcessSteps from "../../../components/ProcessSteps.astro";
import StatStrip from "../../../components/StatStrip.astro";

const fallbackFaqs = [
  { question: "What does an AI automation agency actually build?", answer: "Working software, not slide decks. A good agency builds automated workflows that run inside the tools you already use: email triage that reads and routes messages, invoice processing that extracts and posts data automatically, lead follow-up sequences, approval chains, and data sync between systems. The deliverable is a working automation, tested on your real data, that keeps running after the project ends." },
  { question: "How much does hiring an AI automation agency cost?", answer: "It depends on the number of workflows, the systems involved, and how much data cleanup is needed. A single well-defined workflow, like automated invoice processing, typically costs less than a multi-system integration touching your CRM, ERP, and support inbox at once. Reputable agencies scope the price after a discovery call, not before. Be wary of anyone quoting a number before they've seen your actual workflows." },
  { question: "What's the difference between an AI automation agency and a freelancer?", answer: "A freelancer is one person with a finite set of skills and no backup if they get sick, get busy, or move on. An agency brings a team that covers discovery, build, and ongoing support, plus a process for handling scope changes and post-launch issues. For a single small script, a freelancer might be fine. For anything touching multiple business systems that needs to keep working for years, an agency carries less risk." },
  { question: "How long does an agency engagement typically take?", answer: "Most engagements run from a few weeks to a couple of months, depending on scope. A single workflow with one integration can go live in under a month. Multi-workflow projects touching several systems take longer, mostly because of data mapping and testing on real, messy data rather than a clean demo dataset." },
  { question: "Do I need to replace my current software to work with an AI automation agency?", answer: "No, and you should be skeptical of any agency that tells you otherwise. A competent agency automates on top of your existing CRM, ERP, email, and business tools via API. Replacing your stack is a much bigger, riskier project than automating the manual work sitting inside it." },
  { question: "Can an AI automation agency work with a small team or startup?", answer: "Yes. Agencies scope projects to a single workflow just as often as a multi-system rollout. A five-person company automating lead follow-up doesn't need the same engagement as a 200-person operation automating finance across three systems. The scope and price should match what you actually need, not a fixed package built for a bigger business." },
  { question: "What happens to the automation if we stop working with the agency?", answer: "With a good agency, it keeps running. The automation lives inside your own systems, your CRM, your automation platform account, your integrations, not on infrastructure the agency controls and can shut off. Ask this directly before signing: if we walked away tomorrow, would the automation still work?" },
  { question: "Should the agency have experience in our specific industry?", answer: "It helps, but it's not the deciding factor. The underlying skill, mapping a workflow, handling edge cases, integrating systems through an API, transfers across industries. What matters more is whether they ask good questions about how your business actually works, rather than assuming your process matches a generic template." },
];

let faqItems = fallbackFaqs;

try {
  const supabase = getSupabaseAdmin();
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from('faq_items')
    .select('question, answer, sort_order')
    .eq('section', 'ai_automation_agencies')
    .eq('published', true)
    .order('sort_order');

  if (!error && data && data.length > 0) {
    faqItems = data;
  }
} catch (err) {
  // Fallback to hardcoded content on error
}

const processSteps = [
  { icon: "search", step: "Step 1", title: "Discovery Call", description: "We ask about your current workflows, tools, and where the manual work actually lives. No technical prep needed on your side." },
  { icon: "edit", step: "Step 2", title: "Scoped Proposal", description: "You get a written scope: which workflows, which systems, what it costs, and what 'done' looks like, before any work starts." },
  { icon: "build", step: "Step 3", title: "Build & Test", description: "We build against your real data in a staging environment and fix edge cases before anything touches production." },
  { icon: "rocket_launch", step: "Step 4", title: "Launch & Support", description: "The automation goes live. We monitor it for 90 days and stay reachable if something needs adjusting." },
];

Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 's-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300');

const ldJson = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Service',
    name: 'AI Automation Agency Services',
    url: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-automation/agencies',
    description: 'What an AI automation agency does, how to evaluate one, and how Lenoo AI runs discovery, build, and post-launch support for automation projects in the UAE.',
    provider: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Lenoo AI', url: 'https://lenooai.com' },
    areaServed: 'United Arab Emirates',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', description: 'AI automation agency engagements: scoped and priced after a free discovery call.' },
    serviceType: 'AI Automation Agency',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://lenooai.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'AI Automation', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-automation' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Agencies', item: 'https://lenooai.com/services/ai-automation/agencies' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqItems.map((f: { question: string; answer: string }) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.question,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer },
    })),
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI Automation Agencies: What They Do & How to Choose One | Lenoo AI"
  description="What an AI automation agency actually delivers, how Lenoo AI's process works, and what to evaluate before hiring one in the UAE."
  canonicalPath="/services/ai-automation/agencies"
  arPath="/ar/services/ai-automation/agencies"
  ogImage="/assets/og-services.jpg"
  ldJson={ldJson}
>

<header class="hero-section">
  <div class="container" style="text-align:center;">
    <div style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:rgba(0,227,253,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(0,227,253,0.28);border-radius:100px;padding:6px 16px;color:#a8f2ff;font-size:0.875rem;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1.5rem;" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Home</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services/ai-automation" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">AI Automation</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span>Agencies</div>
    <h1>AI Automation Agencies: <span class="gradient-text">What They Do & How to Choose One</span></h1>
    <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.72); font-size:1.125rem; max-width:640px; margin:1.5rem auto 0; line-height:1.8;">
      Searching for an AI automation agency and not sure what separates a good one from a bad one? Here's what the work actually involves, what to check before you sign anything, and how Lenoo AI, a UAE-based AI automation agency, runs the process end to end.
    </p>
    <div class="trust-chips" style="justify-content:center; margin:1.75rem 0 2rem;">
      <span class="tchip">Scoped and priced after discovery, never before</span>
      <span class="tchip">90-day post-launch support on every project</span>
    </div>
    <div style="display:flex; gap:16px; justify-content:center; flex-wrap:wrap;">
      <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="ai_automation_agencies_hero" class="btn-primary">Book a Free Discovery Call</button>
      <a href="#what-agencies-do" class="btn-outline">See What an Agency Does ↓</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- HERO IMAGE -->
<div style="position:relative; overflow:hidden; height:260px;">
  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1522071820081-009f0129c71c?w=1400&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop" alt="Team reviewing an automation scope during an agency discovery call" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" width="1400" height="260" style="width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 35%;" />
  <div style="position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top,rgba(0,19,32,0.45) 0%,transparent 55%),linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(0,104,117,0.25),rgba(0,37,59,0.40));"></div>
</div>

<!-- STAT BANNER -->
<div style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#00253b,#006875); padding:4rem 0;">
  <div class="container">
    <StatStrip stats={[
      { value: "40%", caption: "of work tasks in most businesses are automatable with existing AI, according to McKinsey research." },
      { value: "90 days", caption: "post-launch monitoring included with every Lenoo AI project" },
      { value: "100%", caption: "refund guarantee if you're not satisfied with what we deliver" },
    ]} />
  </div>
</div>

<!-- WHAT AGENCIES DO -->
<section id="what-agencies-do" class="section" aria-labelledby="what-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="what-heading">What an AI Automation Agency Actually Does</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">The label covers a lot of ground. Here's what the work looks like day to day, broken into the five stages most projects go through.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-3">
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">search</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Discovery</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Understanding how a workflow actually runs today: who touches it, which tools are involved, and where the manual effort piles up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">account_tree</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Workflow Mapping</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Turning a described process into a defined sequence of triggers, decisions, and outputs that software can act on.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">build</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Build</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Writing and testing the automation logic itself, including how it handles the messy, unusual cases, not just the clean examples.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">integration_instructions</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Integration</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Connecting the automation to your existing CRM, ERP, inbox, or spreadsheets via API, so it runs inside the tools your team already uses.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">monitoring</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Monitoring</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Watching the automation once it's live, fixing what breaks, and adjusting it as your process or data changes over time.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">extension</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Works Across Many Tools</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">A good agency isn't tied to one platform. See the <a href="/services/ai-automation/tools">full list of tools we build on</a>, from Zapier and Make to n8n and Power Automate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- DIY VS AGENCY DECISION -->
<section id="agencies-operations" class="section" style="background:#f9f9ff;" aria-labelledby="agencies-operations-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 id="agencies-operations-heading">Not Every Automation Project Needs an Outside Agency</h2>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Some automation work is simple enough to handle with an off-the-shelf tool and a free afternoon. Other projects involve enough custom logic, system integration, or ongoing upkeep that bringing in an agency ends up faster and cheaper than the hours it would otherwise cost internally.</p>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">Knowing which bucket a project falls into before you start evaluating vendors saves time on both sides. A good agency would rather scope real work than talk you out of something you could finish yourself in a weekend.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758518731462-d091b0b4ed0d?w=800&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop" alt="Two businessmen signing a contract at a table while a colleague looks on in a modern office" loading="lazy" width="800" height="600" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- AGENCY PRESENTATION IMAGE BAND -->
<div style="position:relative; overflow:hidden; height:280px; margin:0 0 -1px;">
  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1542744173-8e7e53415bb0?w=1400&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop" alt="Consultant presenting a plan on a screen to a team seated around a table with laptops" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="280" style="width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 35%;" />
  <div style="position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top,rgba(0,19,32,0.35) 0%,transparent 55%);"></div>
</div>

<!-- DIY VS AGENCY TABS -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="agencies-tabs-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="agencies-tabs-heading">Do It Yourself vs. Bring in an Agency</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">A rough guide to which automation projects are worth building in-house, and which ones are worth handing off.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="agencies-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="DIY versus agency guidance">
      <button type="button" id="agencies-tab-diy" class="agencies-tab-btn active" data-agencies-tab="diy" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="agencies-panel-diy">Do It Yourself</button>
      <button type="button" id="agencies-tab-agency" class="agencies-tab-btn" data-agencies-tab="agency" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="agencies-panel-agency">Bring in an Agency</button>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-3" id="agencies-panel-diy" data-agencies-tab-panel="diy" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="agencies-tab-diy">
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">handyman</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">An Off-the-Shelf Tool Already Fits</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">A no-code tool like Zapier or Make already does what you need, with no custom logic or system-specific edge cases to handle.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">shield</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Low Cost If It Breaks</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">The workflow touches one system, and a broken automation is a minor inconvenience to fix, not a business risk while it's down.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">person</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Someone Has the Time</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Someone on the team is comfortable enough with the tools to build and maintain it without it pulling focus from their actual job for long.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-3" id="agencies-panel-agency" data-agencies-tab-panel="agency" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="agencies-tab-agency" hidden>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">hub</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Custom Logic or Multiple Systems</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">The workflow spans several systems or needs custom logic and edge-case handling that off-the-shelf tools can't cleanly support.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">verified_user</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Reliability Actually Matters</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">The process is business-critical enough that downtime or errors carry a real cost, and it needs monitoring and support after launch.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">group_off</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">No Internal Bandwidth</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Nobody on the team has the time or technical background to build and maintain it properly, and hiring for it isn't worth a full role.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- GOOD VS BAD -->
<section class="section" style="background:#f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="good-bad-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="good-bad-heading">What Separates a Good Agency From a Bad One</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">The label "AI automation agency" gets applied loosely. These four things are what actually distinguish a good engagement from a bad one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-2">
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">support_agent</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Post-Launch Support</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Automation breaks when your data or process shifts. A good agency includes a monitoring period after launch. A bad one hands you a file and disappears the day it goes live.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">hub</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Works With Your Tools, Not Against Them</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">A good agency builds on top of your existing CRM, ERP, and email via API. Be cautious of anyone who wants to migrate you to a new platform before they've even scoped the automation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">receipt_long</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Transparent Scoping</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">You should get a written scope of exactly which workflows are covered, what the price is, and what "done" means, before any work begins. Vague quotes lead to vague deliverables.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">lock_open</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">No Lock-In</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">You should own the automation and understand how it works, not depend permanently on the agency to touch it. If a provider won't explain their build or hands you nothing you can hand off later, that's a red flag.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKLIST -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="checklist-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:760px; margin:0 auto;">
      <div class="section-header">
        <h2 id="checklist-heading">What to Ask Before Hiring an AI Automation Agency</h2>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">Nine questions worth asking any agency before you sign anything, and why each one actually matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tools-strip" style="text-align:left;">
        <ul style="list-style:none; margin:0; padding:0; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:1.5rem;">
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>Will this run on top of our existing tools, or do we need to switch systems first?</strong><br />Migrating platforms is a much bigger project than automating the workflow inside them. If the answer depends on replacing your CRM or email provider first, that's added cost and risk you didn't ask for, and a sign the agency doesn't build flexibly around what you already have.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>What happens when the automation encounters a case it wasn't built for?</strong><br />Real data is messy: an invoice missing a field, a lead with no email, an approval that needs an exception. Ask specifically how the automation flags these cases for a human to review, rather than failing silently or guessing at an answer.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>Is there a support period after launch, and what does it cover?</strong><br />Workflows shift as your business changes: a new field gets added to a form, a supplier changes their invoice format, a tool updates its API. Find out exactly how long support runs and whether it covers these kinds of adjustments or only outright bugs.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>Can you show me a written scope before I commit to anything?</strong><br />A written scope is what turns a sales conversation into an accountable agreement. If an agency resists putting the workflows, systems, timeline, and price in writing before you sign, there's nothing to hold them to later.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>Who owns the automation once it's built, and can we maintain it ourselves if we need to?</strong><br />You want the underlying logic to belong to you, not to sit permanently inside the agency's private tooling. Ask whether you'd be able to hand the automation to another developer or team if you ever needed to.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>What happens if we're not satisfied with the result?</strong><br />This is where you find out what the agency's confidence actually looks like in writing. A refund policy, a revision period, or a satisfaction guarantee tells you they expect to deliver. A shrug tells you they don't.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>Can I see an example of a similar workflow you've built before, even redacted?</strong><br />Past work, even with client names and numbers removed, tells you whether the agency has actually solved a comparable problem before or is learning the ropes on your project and your budget.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>How do you handle data access and privacy while the project is being built?</strong><br />Giving an outside team API access to your CRM or finance tools is a real security decision. Ask how credentials are stored, whether access is revoked once the project ends, and who on their side can actually see your data during testing.</span></li>
          <li style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; align-items:flex-start;"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:20px; margin-top:2px;">check_circle</span><span style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.9375rem; line-height:1.7;"><strong>What's the total cost, including any ongoing fees, not just the build?</strong><br />Some agencies quote a low build price and then add hosting, API, or maintenance fees that were never mentioned upfront. Get the full picture, build cost plus any recurring cost, before you sign anything.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- RED FLAGS -->
<section class="section" style="background:#f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="red-flags-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="red-flags-heading">Red Flags: Signs an Automation Agency Won't Deliver</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">The questions above surface most of these on their own. Here's what to watch for if the answers feel evasive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-3">
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">help</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Vague Scoping</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">If an agency can't describe exactly which workflows they'll automate and what "done" looks like before you sign, expect scope creep, missed expectations, or a project that quietly narrows once the invoice is paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">block</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">No Post-Launch Support</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Automations aren't set-and-forget. Data formats change, APIs get updated, business rules shift. An agency that disappears the day the project goes live is handing you a liability, not a finished product.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">lock</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Proprietary Lock-In</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Some agencies build on closed, in-house tooling you can't access, edit, or take with you. If you can't move the automation to another provider without starting over, you don't own it. You're renting it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">visibility_off</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">No Portfolio or References</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">An agency that can't point to anything they've built before, even under NDA with details removed, may not have done this at your scale. Ask for a walkthrough of a comparable project or a reference call.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">schedule</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Pressure to Sign Fast</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">A rushed decision on a multi-week engagement rarely goes well. Time-limited discounts or pressure to commit before you've seen a written scope are sales tactics, not signs of a well-run agency.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">payments</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Full Payment Upfront</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">Legitimate agencies scope the work before pricing it, and most split payment across milestones rather than asking for the full amount before any work has started. Full payment upfront removes their incentive to finish.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PRICING MODELS -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="pricing-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="pricing-heading">How AI Automation Agencies Typically Price Their Work</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">Most agencies use one of three pricing models, or a mix. Knowing the difference helps you judge whether a proposal fits your situation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-3">
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">request_quote</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Fixed-Scope Project</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">You agree on a defined set of workflows, a fixed price, and a delivery timeline before work starts. This is the most common model for a single workflow or a small, related group of them, because both sides know exactly what they're buying. It works best when the process is well understood and unlikely to change mid-project.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">event_repeat</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Retainer</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">You pay a recurring monthly fee for ongoing automation work: new workflows, adjustments to existing ones, and continued monitoring. This suits businesses that expect to keep automating over time rather than complete one project and stop, since it avoids re-negotiating scope every time something new comes up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-card">
        <div class="service-icon"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">schedule</span></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.0625rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.625rem;">Hourly / Time & Materials</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7; margin:0;">You pay for actual hours worked rather than a fixed deliverable. This can make sense for exploratory work where the scope genuinely isn't clear yet, but it shifts the cost risk onto you if the project runs long. Ask for a not-to-exceed cap if an agency proposes this for a defined project.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; max-width:800px; margin:2rem auto 0; text-align:center;">
      Which model fits depends on how well-defined your automation need is. A single, clear workflow suits a fixed price. An open-ended need for automation across a growing business suits a retainer. Be cautious of an agency that pushes hourly billing on a project with a clear, contained scope, that's usually a sign the price is being kept deliberately soft rather than committed to.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

